<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE amazon-leo-lv01 ascent (3DOF sim)</name><description>ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Vulcan VC6L Amazon Leo LV-01 (SLC-41) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
Method: first-principles 3DOF sim (/workspace/trajectory-deck/sim/ascent3dof.py): WGS-84 rotating Earth, J2, US76 drag,
TIMELINE mode: every ignition/cutoff/separation/fairing time is a published hard event; mdot=F_vac/(Isp_vac*g0); thrust SL/vac on ambient pressure.
FITTED (least squares vs published knots): vertical rise 7.22 s, kick 8.812 deg over 10 s, Cd 0.400, payload 20280 kg,
gravity turn, then linear-tangent steering tan(theta)=-0.3730--0.002067*(t-t_ign stage 2).
Vehicle config: ULA Vulcan VC6L (2x BE-4, 6x GEM 63XL, LEO-optimized Centaur V / CV-L with 2x RL10C-1-1A, 21.3 m fairing) — TIMELINE mode (sim/vehicles/vulcan_vc6l.json; every number carries a source URL, derivation, or ASSUMPTION).
Vulcan VC6L: 6 GEM 63XL (NG catalog p.39 masses, 87.3 s burn, 334,063 lbf vacuum time-average thrust, Isp 280.3 s), 2×BE-4 550k-lbf SL (ULA UG / Vulcan Leo 1 page), LEO-optimized Centaur V (ULA: 35 ft, a third less propellant than the 120,000-lb CV-HE, twin RL10C-1-1A at 23,825 lbf each), 21.3 m fairing (ULA).
TIMELINE mode: booster/Centaur event times from ULA UG Fig. 2.1.1-2 (CCSFS two-burn example, &#x27;Approximate Times &amp; Values&#x27;) plus NG 87.3 s SRB burn and UG &#x27;~100 s&#x27; SRB jettison — TYPICAL sequence, not LV-01&#x27;s unpublished timeline. MES1 cutoff is an ASSUMED duration, tuned so the first burn targets a 180 × 450 km transfer.
BE-4 Isp, booster dry mass, CV-L dry mass and fairing mass are secondary or ASSUMED (see fields). BE-4 throttle to 4.6 g (UG) is not modelled; full published sea-level/derived-vac thrust is used throughout the booster phase.
PRE-FLIGHT ILLUSTRATIVE. ULA Vulcan VC6L, first 6-SRB / first LEO-optimized Centaur V flight, 40 Amazon Leo satellites from SLC-41, T0 2026-11-13T17:54:00Z (Fri Nov 13 9:54 a.m. PST).
Booster/Centaur event times are ULA UG Fig. 2.1.1-2 TYPICAL CCSFS values (Approximate Times), not this flight. MES1 cutoff is an ASSUMED 600 s burn (T+908 s), tuned toward a 180 × 450 km transfer. MES2 (circularize to 450 km) is modelled at first apoapsis in post_cutoff. Deploy time for LV-01 is not in a first-hand source retrieved this turn, so after MES2 the stage is propagated +2 revs on the inferred insertion orbit.
Target (see launch config): insertion 180 km, apoapsis 450 km, fpa 0, launch azimuth 43.69 deg; payload 20280 kg.
Pad: 28.583410N -80.583036E. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s (Earth-relative).
T0 used: 2026-11-13T17:54:00Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.
POST-CUTOFF: deploy time unpublished (LV-01 payload-separation time is not in a first-hand ULA/Amazon/FCC document retrieved this turn (a NSF-forum quotation of a ULA STA giving first sep at ~1 h 17 min was not independently verified). After MES2 the stage is propagated 2 revs on the inferred 450 km insertion orbit.). From after last burn T+3637.5 s the stage is propagated for TWO revolutions (osculating period 93.77 min) on the inferred insertion orbit (same WGS-84/J2/US76-drag model); rows tagged -coast/-burn.
POST-CUTOFF burn &#x27;MES2 (Centaur V circularization)&#x27;: ign T+3624.9 s, 12.6 s at 211.97 kN prograde (Isp 453.8 s); LV-01 burn 2 time unpublished. ULA UG: one or two Centaur burns are typical for LEO. MES2 is flown at first apoapsis after the ASSUMED 180 × 450 km MES1 transfer, open thrust, duration solved to 450 km perigee (circularize).
POST-CUTOFF end T+14890 s: inferred insertion orbit, +2 revs (deploy time unpublished): alt 479.81 km, osculating 450.0 x 468.0 km, i 49.66 deg.
POST-CUTOFF note: Amazon KA-01 (Atlas V, 28 Apr 2025, same pad) deployed at 450 km / 280 miles (Amazon). NSF reported that insertion as 450 km circular, 51.9 deg. This LV-01 sim uses 450 km as the insertion target and the NAVAREA IV 1078/26 corridor azimuth (i~52.7 deg) without retuning.
POST-CUTOFF note: Centaur disposal (HYDROPAC 3246/26 Eastern Indian Ocean, ~T+2.5 h, matching the FAA-window backups) is not modelled; the file ends 2 revs after MES2.</description>
